 ## Background
-The problem of topological ambiguity in Marching Cubes was first identified by Lorensen and Cline (1987), who proposed 15 unambiguous cases, leaving 18 ambiguous cases unresolved [774-marching-cubes.md]. Natarajan (1995) provided a rigorous test for these ambiguities using the trilinear interpolant [790-asymptotic-decider.md]. Subsequent work by Chernyaev (MC33) and others expanded the lookup tables to handle all 33 topological cases [1618-extended-trilinear-triangulation.md]. For binary volumes, combinatorial topology approaches by Coeurjolly et al. established the use of connectedness couples to guarantee manifold surfaces [1428-topologically-defined-isosurfaces.md]. Recent developments integrate these guarantees into differentiable rendering pipelines using consistency losses [4351-topology-normal-consistency-loss.md].
+All methods in this family address the "ambiguous face" and "interior tunnel" cases where the sign pattern of voxel corners does not uniquely determine the surface topology under linear interpolation [G]. The algorithms identify configurations where the standard 15-case table is insufficient [G]. This typically occurs when a face has alternating signs (a saddle) or when the interior of the cube contains a critical point [Asymptotic Decider](790-asymptotic-decider.md)[Topologically Correct Marching Cubes](1368-topologically-correct-marching-cubes.md). Resolution strategies vary: geometric tests evaluate the bilinear interpolant on ambiguous faces [Asymptotic Decider](790-asymptotic-decider.md); vertex insertion adds anchors (e.g., at the cell center) to resolve interior tunnels [Topologically Correct Marching Cubes](1368-topologically-correct-marching-cubes.md); connectivity constraints enforce valid foreground/background couples for binary data [Topologically Defined Isosurfaces](1428-topologically-defined-isosurfaces.md); and higher-order interpolation uses Hermite data or averaged face values [Marching Cubes with Hermite Data and Full Case Table](961-marching-cubes-with-hermite-data-and-full-case-table.md). The primary goal is to produce a closed, oriented 2-manifold mesh [G]. This requires handling degenerate cases (e.g., $Q=0$) and ensuring that shared edges between adjacent voxels are triangulated consistently [Topologically Correct Marching Cubes](1368-topologically-correct-marching-cubes.md)[Topologically Defined Isosurface via Connectedness Couples](1430-topologically-defined-isosurface-via-connectedness-couples.md).
 
